.TH SEEK 2
.SH NAME
seek \- change file offset
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "seek :: proc(fd: int, offset: u64) -> i64"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Seek
sets the descriptor's cursor to the absolute byte offset
.IR offset ,
and answers the new offset.
.PP
Unlike Plan 9's
.IR seek ,
there is no
.I whence
argument: every call names an offset from the start of the file.
A program that wants to seek relative to the current offset or to the
file's length asks for those first, with
.I stat
(see
.IR stat (2))
or by tracking what it has already transferred, and computes the new
offset before calling
.IR seek .
.PP
The offset is the descriptor's own, not the file's: two descriptors open
on the same file keep separate cursors, even when they were made with
.IR dup (2).
.I Seek
does not check what kind of file the descriptor names; a directory's
offset is a cookie from
.I dirread
(see
.IR stat (2))
rather than a byte count, and a pipe has no notion of position that a seek
can usefully change.
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/sys.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/syscall.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (2),
.IR open (2),
.IR read (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Answers the new offset on success and a negative
.B \-errno
on failure; see
.IR errstr (2).
.B EBADF
is a descriptor that is not open.
